I'm going to cover several techniques  that can help improve the look of your  procedural terrain specifically we'll  talk about more advanced blending  techniques try planar mapping splatting  and texture repetition we're continuing  the series on 3d world generation in  this project and talking a bit about  texturing strategies there's a reason  the big AAA games go to real lengths to  get the terrain looking good it's a  complex subject and there's a lot of  ways to do this we previously covered  quadtrees and planetary level of detail  but up until this point we sort of  skirted the issue of textures and  shaders on the train in favor of mesh  generation what I'm going to do today is  cover a few different approaches to  terrain texturing a sort of  mix-and-match of techniques that you can  be familiar with and you can decide  whether or not they're right for your  project  so those techniques are tri planar  mapping texture splatting height based  blending and texture repetition and what  I'm going to do is go over each of these  in a bit of detail remember that the  code is all available as well if you  need to reference it feel free let's  start with tri planar mapping so let's  look at what this technique is trying to  fix we've got some simple rolling hills  and we choose the UVs based on the XY  coordinates so once I add a texture it  all looks textured now if I were to  sharply increase the height of the  terrain say for a cliff face or  something like that notice what happens  you've got this ugly stretching right  here on the cliff face and that's  because we're essentially projecting a  texture down from above and this is  exactly the problem we're looking to  solve we want that texture to be applied  appropriately from every angle not just  above this is where try planar mapping  comes in the idea behind it is really  simple sample the texture three times  once along each axis and then projected  onto your geometry along that axis and  just kind of blend based on which way  it's facing going back to this piece of  the landscape now if you were to sample  the texture now not just from above but  from each side and then blend them all  together this would look a lot better  let's take a quick look at the shader  code that makes this happen what I do  here is I use the world space position  of  each fragment and then we take the  normal here and use this as the waiting  for each texture sample one thing I do  need to call out try planar mapping for  the diffuse is really easy but it's a  little trickier to get the lighting  right and that's because you need to  construct your tangent properly then  golus has a nice write-up that I'll  include in the description that goes  into depth but it's pretty much just  constructing the TBN matrices for each  normal sample and then doing the same  try blending we were doing for the  diffuse once we get that all going you  can see that everything kind of looks  right from every angle the second  technique we'll talk about is texture  splatting and this is conceptually  really simple what you're basically  doing is blending between multiple  textures using some other source of data  imagine here I've got two textures so  I've got this grass and I've got this  stone texture and I've got that  landscape from before where all the  middle will be stone  imagine I go and create a third texture  just black and white and then on the  terrain I just blend between the grass  or the rocks depending on whether the  texture is black or white this is in  essence what texture spotting is now  since the texture can have four channels  rgba I could make my blend texture even  more advanced by storing blending  weights in each Channel and then my  shader could blend between four  different textures based on that this is  all pretty much what texture splatting  is blending between a bunch of different  textures and with that in mind I'm gonna  race ahead to a much more flexible  version that I've added here complete  with source code what I do is instead of  having fixed textures in the shader I  buy in just a single texture array and  what we'll do here is we'll load a whole  bunch of textures here I've got eight  different textures that will mash  together into a single texture array and  then for each vertex will pass down four  pairs of values those will be the index  of each texture and the weight so as we  generate each vertex on the terrain will  also decide what textures to apply here  in this get texture weights function I  have a dictionary of all possible  textures and then I just apply weights  depending on the height moisture levels  whatever will use this over here in the  terrain chunk so will generate the  texture weight pairs and then this is  important so make sure you do this you  have to  get the textures the same per face and  then we passed those down into the  vertex shader the vertex shader just  passes those through to the fragment  shader and then here in the fragment  shader we can loop over the four  textures  sample them add them all together  weighted by the weight values once we've  done that we've got textures all over  the terrain and we can use as many as we  want on the terrain as long as you don't  have more than four in one spot moving  on let's talk about how to effectively  blend between textures on terrain now  normally when you blend from one texture  to another you just use a simple alpha  base blending so you get a super smooth  transition from one side to the next  this is really easy to implement but if  you want to take this just a little bit  further you want to use what's called  height based blending there's a great  article by Andrei Mishkin as' that I'll  link in the description but the gist of  the technique is that you incorporate  height into the basic alpha blending  calculation the end result being that  let's say you're blending from grass to  rocks you get grass poking out between  the rocks instead of a straight linear  blend of course to make it work with the  texture splatting that we did above we  need to modify it so I do that in  terrain blend underscore four to blend  for sample simultaneously since we need  the result to be order independent now  let's move on to the last technique here  a pseudo texture bombing technique so  first let's talk about the problem being  solved here looking at the landscape  will disable all the splatting and  triplane or stuff and here's a single  texture applied over the whole landscape  notice the distinctive pattern that  appears and it doesn't matter which  texture we choose the pattern will be  there it's really difficult to get rid  of and that's because if you look at the  UVs we've just tiled everything in every  direction it almost looks like a  checkerboard  now texture bombing is a technique from  Nvidia from like 2004 and it's basically  just a way of breaking up that  repetition they go a little nuts  sampling the texture four times but the  results are good luckily Inigo keela's  has another technique on his website  again linked below that uses a clever  trick that uses 8 virtually tiled  versions of the texture and interpolates  between 2  them so the shader code honestly I've  just lifted this straight off any ghost  site since it worked out of the box and  you can see here as I enable it the  pattern is a lot less distinct and there  we have it some techniques for texturing  our procedural world we can mix and  match between the techniques too so you  can enable just one or two here I've  just got per triplane err with a single  texture no splatting and the texture  repetition trick this would look great  as some sort of rocky world or moon for  example the point here is to know and  understand the techniques at your  disposal source code is all available on  github so fork it and go ahead and tweak  it or add to it do whatever you want  make sure you hit like and subscribe and  I'll see you next time Cheers

